\chapter{Introduction}

FitzCLIM is a Common Lisp implementation of the \textsc{Clim}~II
protocols~\cite{clim-ii} and of the \textsc{Clim}~2.2 revisions.  The
2.2 revisions are recorded in Franz's \textsc{Clim}~2.2 User
Guide~\cite{clim-22-ug}, not in a second formal specification.  That
guide is copyrighted.  This manual does not copy it.  FitzCLIM is aimed
at \textsc{Sbcl}.

The frame manager realizes the standard gadgets as native controls.  On
Windows those are Windows controls.  On macOS those are Cocoa controls.
Layout positions them through the sheet protocol.  A native control does not
hold output records.  Its items may still be Lisp objects, and a selection
may run a command.

An application pane is drawn by FitzCLIM.  It records output and holds
presentations.  A command loop reads commands, from typing or from a gesture
on a presentation, and runs them.  After a command, panes that have a
display function are drawn again.

\texttt{define-application-frame} builds the frame class and its command
table.  \texttt{:command-definer} names the macro that adds a command to
that table.  The default name is \texttt{define-\textit{frame}-command}.
\texttt{:menu-bar} chooses the command table shown as the menu bar, or
\texttt{nil} for none.  \texttt{:disabled-commands} names commands that
start disabled.  \texttt{command-enabled} turns one on or off.
\texttt{:top-level} names the function that runs the command loop.
A command argument may name a \texttt{:gesture}.  That gesture on a
presentation of the argument's type runs the command.  The other
arguments take their \texttt{:default} values.  A command line that
ends early uses those defaults too.  \texttt{:keystroke}
on the command is a character that runs it from the menu.
\texttt{:pane} supplies one pane.  \texttt{:panes} and \texttt{:layouts}
supply named panes and more than one arrangement.

A 3D pane is a native OpenGL child window, a sibling of the native controls.
Pointer events at its edge become a ray.  A hit returns a Lisp object and
enters the same command and translator machinery as a 2D presentation.  The
OpenGL pane owns its redraw.  FitzCLIM may ask that pane to repaint.  The
pane does not record its scene as output records.  A label in the scene is
drawn and picked there.

The 2D graphics protocol stays two-dimensional.  Regions, transforms,
clipping, formatted output, and output recording are a plane.  They stay out
of the scene graph.

FitzCLIM was begun so that \textsc{Edefb}~\cite{edefb}, the Elite Dangerous
Electronic Flight Bag, can have a \textsc{Clim}.  The toolkit is general.
Other programs will use it too.  Elite Dangerous stays in the \textsc{Edefb}
repository.

This is a clean-room implementation.  It is not derived from McCLIM, from
Franz \textsc{Clim}, or from LispWorks \textsc{Clim}.  The specification may
be consulted.  Its text stays out of this tree.

Tests use $\Sigma$~\cite{gore-sigma}, in particular the \texttt{behavior}
and \texttt{should} forms in \texttt{sigma/behave}.

The frame managers target Windows and macOS.  Geometry, ink, sheets,
commands, frames, events, presentations, output recording, gadgets,
pane layout, and the frame managers are under \texttt{source/}.
An application pane records what it draws and finds the presentation
under a pointer.  A frame can stack its panes.  A gadget holds a value
or runs a callback.  On Windows the frame manager realizes that frame
as a window and those gadgets as native controls.  Pointer and keyboard
input on an application pane become CLIM events, and a paint replays
the recorded output.  On macOS the frame manager realizes the frame as
a Cocoa window and the gadgets as Cocoa controls.  Pointer and keyboard
input become CLIM events, and a menu of the frame's commands is installed
in the menu bar.

You can download the library from GitHub at:\\
\url{https://github.com/cgore/FitzCLIM}

\section*{Getting Lisp}

Before using this library you need a working Lisp.  I use and recommend
\textsc{Sbcl}, Steel Bank Common Lisp, which is available at:\\
\url{http://www.sbcl.org}

\section*{Getting \textsc{Emacs} and \textsc{Slime}}

The best way to interact with any Common Lisp is via \textsc{Slime}, the
Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for \textsc{Emacs}.  It is available at:\\
\url{https://common-lisp.net/project/slime/}
